 ## Overview
-Interval Shading is a volume rendering technique that uses tetrahedra as primitives, rasterized via mesh shaders to generate depth intervals (front and back face depths) for each pixel, allowing parallel evaluation of volume properties like optical depth or ray marching steps without order-dependence or acceleration structures [S1]. Unlike [Shading and Frame Buffer Filling](1808-shading-and-frame-buffer-filling.md) which may rely on traditional depth buffers or [Volume Raycasting with Surface Extraction and Phong Shading](3662-volume-raycasting-with-surface-extraction-and-phong-shading.md) which often requires acceleration structures like BVHs, Interval Shading coerces the rasterizer to compute two depths per fragment, enabling exact, order-independent transparency and distributed ray marching in a single pass [S1].
+Interval Shading is a volume rendering technique that rasterizes tetrahedra via mesh shaders to generate depth intervals (front and back face depths) per fragment, enabling parallel evaluation of optical depth or ray marching without acceleration structures [S1]. Unlike [Shading and Frame Buffer Filling](1808-shading-and-frame-buffer-filling.md) which uses traditional depth buffers, or [Volume Raycasting with Surface Extraction and Phong Shading](3662-volume-raycasting-with-surface-extraction-and-phong-shading.md) which relies on acceleration structures, Interval Shading coerces the rasterizer to compute two depths per fragment in a single pass [S1].
 
